      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let Jane's current age be \\( x \\). According to the problem, one year ago, her age was a perfect square, and one year from now, her age will be a perfect cube. Therefore, we can write the following equations:\n\\[ x - 1 = n^2 \\]\n\\[ x + 1 = m^3 \\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is an integer and \\( m \\) is an integer.\n\nFrom the first equation, we can express \\( x \\) as:\n\\[ x = n^2 + 1 \\]\n\nSubstituting this expression for \\( x \\) into the second equation, we get:\n\\[ n^2 + 1 + 1 = m^3 \\]\n\\[ n^2 + 2 = m^3 \\]\n\\[ m^3 = n^2 + 2 \\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions to this equation. Let's test some values for \\( n \\):\n\n1. If \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[ m^3 = 1^2 + 2 = 3 \\]\n   This is not a perfect cube.\n\n2. If \\( n = 2 \\):\n   \\[ m^3 = 2^2 + 2 = 6 \\]\n   This is not a perfect cube.\n\n3. If \\( n = 3 \\):\n   \\[ m^3 = 3^2 + 2 = 11 \\]\n   This is not a perfect cube.\n\n4. If \\( n = 4 \\):\n   \\[ m^3 = 4^2 + 2 = 18 \\]\n   This is not a perfect cube.\n\n5. If \\( n = 5 \\):\n   \\[ m^3 = 5^2 + 2 = 27 \\]\n   This is a perfect cube (\\( m = 3 \\)).\n\nSo, \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( m = 3 \\) is a solution. Substituting \\( n = 5 \\) back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = n^2 + 1 = 5^2 + 1 = 25 + 1 = 26 \\]\n\nTo verify, we check the conditions of the problem:\n- One year ago, Jane's age was \\( 26 - 1 = 25 \\), which is a perfect square (\\( 5^2 \\)).\n- One year from now, Jane's age will be \\( 26 + 1 = 27 \\), which is a perfect cube (\\( 3^3 \\)).\n\nTherefore, Jane is currently \\(\\boxed{26}\\) years old.",
